The accurate DCT at every quality. ImageMagick wrote a JPEG of -quality 90 or less with the
fast integer DCT of the library (JDCT_IFAST), the least accurate one, and took the accurate
one (JDCT_ISLOW) only above 90. With mozjpeg the fast DCT costs bytes at the same quality:
about 1% at -quality 80, about 9% at 90 (the formats of the screenshots of a video, median
of 45 frames and sizes, SSIMULACRA2, 25.11.2026). Now the DCT of the library (ISLOW) at
every quality (aZ, 25.11.2026 19:34: «да, и в преимущество это»); -define
jpeg:dct-method=ifast (or fastest) still takes the fast one. A picture that is not a JPEG
then gives the same bytes as the encoder libjpeg of our ffmpeg with its defaults (-dct
islow, -sampling 420) at the same quality below 90.

--- a/coders/jpeg.c
+++ b/coders/jpeg.c
@@ -3291,8 +3291,10 @@
       extent_info=DestroyImageInfo(extent_info);
     }
   jpeg_set_quality(jpeg_info,quality,TRUE);
-  if ((dct_method == (const char *) NULL) && (quality <= 90))
-    jpeg_info->dct_method=JDCT_IFAST;
+  /*
+    The accurate DCT of the library at every quality, not the fast one up to
+    90: -define jpeg:dct-method=ifast still takes that.
+  */
 #if (JPEG_LIB_VERSION >= 70)
   option=GetImageOption(image_info,"quality");
   if (option != (const char *) NULL)
